      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the low murmur of conversation and the clink of cups. Morning air hits your face \u2014 damp, carrying the smell of wet stone and something faintly metallic, like old iron after rain.\n\nThe street slopes downward, past shuttered windows and empty doorways. The buildings grow sparser, more weathered, as if the town itself has been pulling back from something. A few stray papers skitter across the cobbles ahead of you, driven by a wind that seems to come from nowhere.\n\nAnd then you see it.\n\nA cracked black arch stands alone against the grey sky, perhaps forty paces ahead. No wall brackets it, no building frames it \u2014 it simply *rises* from the earth like a rib bone, its surface veined with pale scars where the stone has split and been mortared back. A heavy iron gate fills the arch's mouth, its bars thick as a man's forearm, bound together by a massive lock of tarnished brass.\n\nThe mechanism gleams dully \u2014 a complex nest of gears and tumblers visible through a glass faceplate, frozen mid-kiss. The Kestrel Guild's seal, a bird in flight, is stamped into the brass.\n\nNo one stands watch. No one moves nearby. The gate is simply... there. Silent. Waiting.\n\nUp close, you notice something at its base \u2014 a scrap of dark fabric, snagged on a jagged spur of stone. Frayed. A few days old, maybe."
    },
