      "narrative": "You crouch low, running your fingers through the loose dust and rubble that carpets the base of the arch. The stone grit feels ordinary beneath your touch \u2014 nothing disturbed, nothing hidden. You sweep a hand across the ground, sift through the debris, and find... nothing. Just dirt. Just pebbles. Just the same undisturbed scatter that's been here for days.\n\nYour eyes drift to the snagged fabric scrap. You pick it up, turn it over. Dark cloth, frayed at the edges, nondescript. Could be from any traveller's cloak. Could be nothing.\n\nBut the unease doesn't leave you. You feel it prickling at the back of your neck \u2014 the certainty that you've missed something obvious, that the answer was right under your fingers and you couldn't see it.\n\nA sound behind you.\n\nThe rustle of movement from the collapsed wall shadow. The figure pressed there shifts \u2014 a scrape of boot leather, the creak of a strained floorboard. They're not hiding anymore. They're *moving*."
